# Bytes a rule of Polish's names by address, which no lifter writes: these are
# ours. A string a rule holds against the text is one code per character in
# the alphabet the statement type declares, not ASCII, and
# tools/lang-alphabet.py show plpl is what says which code is which letter.
# The phonemes are the phone statement's own alphabet, which
# tools/lang-phonemes.py prints.

# The eight letters Italian has not got, by the codes the alphabet gives them.
bytes pol_letter_aogonek  cf      # a with an ogonek
bytes pol_letter_cacute   d0      # c with an acute
bytes pol_letter_eogonek  d1      # e with an ogonek
bytes pol_letter_lstroke  d2      # l with a stroke
bytes pol_letter_nacute   d3      # n with an acute
bytes pol_letter_sacute   d4      # s with an acute
bytes pol_letter_zacute   d5      # z with an acute
bytes pol_letter_zdot     d6      # z with a dot above

# What the two ogoneks say. They are nasal vowels, which this inventory has
# not got: they are spoken as the vowel and then an n, which is what Polish
# itself does before a stop and near enough elsewhere until there is a nasal
# vowel to use. The other six letters say one phoneme each and the module
# already has a wrapper that lays each of those down, so they name nothing
# here.
bytes pol_ph_on           21 12   # the open o, then n
bytes pol_ph_en           1d 12   # the open e, then n

# The hard affricates, cz and dż. Polish's soft ć and dź are Italian's own C
# and J, which its phonology already breaks into a stop and the palatal
# fricative; the hard pair has no code of its own left, so a letter rule lays
# down the stop and the retroflex fricative itself, which is the same two
# sounds in the same order.
bytes pol_ph_tsz          04 18   # t, then the hard sz
bytes pol_ph_dzz          03 16   # d, then the hard ż
